From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 06 Aug 94 15:45:32 EDT
Subject: [#94-8-776169962] Individualism vs Groupism

Nick Szabo wrote 8/6/94: <Credentials are one of the main ways we communicate
reputation in a world bigger than a small tribe.  A credentialing mechanism
forms the centerpiece of David Chaum's privacy-enhanced social mileau, the
inspiriation for crypto-anarchy.  Perhaps your quarrel is with licensing
(monopolistic and legalized credentialism)?>

  People prone to Groupism rather than Individualism, when faced with
uncomfortable facts or differing values and opinions have a strong tendency to
take the following steps: (1) ignore and hope it goes away; (2) if this doesn't
work, attack the credentials of the disagreeable person (who are you?  where did
you go to school?  what groups do you belong to?); (3) if this doesn't work, get
vile; (4) if this doesn't work, either try to take group action against the
offending person or leave the field with tail between legs.  For example, if I
were a credentialist, I might point out to you that you spelled "milieu"
incorrectly, and I might ask you what your credentials in spelling were and
where were you educated?

  Step no. 2 is usually the most successful step, this is why credentialism is
so debilitating to civic discourse.  Individuals today all too often feel they
are just a mote in an inconceivably large mass of people.  People feel unless
they have the authority of some group to back up their own thoughts, that they
ought to shut up when attacked.  Belonging to a group means you don't have to
think through your beliefs, it's the easy way out.  Liberty, historically
brought about by reliance on rationality, leads to a reduction of independent,
rational thought.  Liberty fosters groups because of its core dependence upon an
idea of equality, equality horribly diminishes the individual.  Rationality has
historically always led to relativism, then reason is abandoned and relativistic
ideas are held dogmatically.  In our egalitarian Western Civilization, the
rational self-knowledge of just how weak individuals are, leads to an easier
acceptance of teachings that show how only in groups do we have power.
Individuals find it extremely difficult to believe that they can, in fact, move
the world.  They think they need credentials, when they really don't.

  Licensing, in the form you speak of above, is a necessary evil of modern life.
Necessary because there must be some supervision of standards for professions or
there is no concept of profession left.  Evil because the supervision inevitably
censors not just kooky opinions and incompetence, but learning and ability that
falls outside dogmatic channels.  Thought and observation is only allowed within
predetermined constraints generally based upon dogmatic, half-baked metaphysical
assumptions at the base of all professions.  Alfred North Whitehead would add:
"The intimate timidity of professionalized scholarship circumscribes reason by
reducing its topics to triviality, for example, to bare sensa and to
tautologies.  It then frees itself from criticism by dogmatically handing over
the remainder of experience to an animal faith or a religious mysticism,
incapable of rationalization."

  Modern universities no longer have any core purpose to education, they are
victims of rationally-arrived-at dogmatic relativism.  They are simply
amalgamations of politically-driven courses drifting with the latest pressure
groups and funding battles.  A degree says only that you have given them enough
money, and put up with their indoctrination long enough.  It does not say you
are educated or even trained any more.  It doesn't even guarantee that you can
spell.  It is a group symbol.  Modern professional associations are almost
universally politically oriented towards group goals, not towards individual
excellence.

  The promise in credentialing mechanisms is what you refer to above as the
inspiration for crypto-anarchy.  The danger is what I referred to at the Extro-1
conference, this form of credentialism by a self-selected group mechanism
narrows the focus of criticism, not in the service of truth but in the service
of symbolic utility.  By symbolic utility, I mean focusing on the symbols
themselves, instead of the underlying truth they represent (as revealed by
evidence and causality).  This danger is similar to what occurs if PCR
(Pan-Critical Rationalism) is not grounded in the subjective purpose of getting
at the truth.  If PCR is grounded only in criticism, it devolves to smug
nihilism because of its reliance on others' criticism to define knowledge for
you, the appeal is to authority in the form of credentialism.  Not everyone,
even under ideal conditions, has a strong subjective purpose of getting at the
truth, this warps self-selected credentialing mechanisms.  The mechanisms do not
self-correct because self-selected groups cannot purge warp-inducing members
without the corrupting tinge of statist methods.  Allan Bloom would add this
about group dynamics: "So unless there is some strong ground for opposition to
majority opinion, it inevitably prevails.  This is the really dangerous form of
the tyranny of the majority, not the kind that actively persecutes minorities
but the kind that breaks the inner will to resist because there is no qualified
source of nonconforming principles and no sense of superior right.  The majority
is all there is."

Nick again: <Isn't absolutist individualism a form of ideological purity?>

Every individual qua individual, is an absolute, that is, unique.  Absolutist
individualism seems to me to be a redundant phrase.  Of course, I don't mean to
suggest that individuals exist in a vacuum, they are absolutely connected to
everyone and everything else.

Nancy Lebovitz wrote 8/6/94: <I believe that part of not being a groupist is
checking oneself for any groupist preconceptions that one may have picked up and
working to undo them>

I believe this is what PCR aims for.  Holding open to criticism beliefs brought
into thought by the symbolic utility that groups peddle.

John Bozeman wrote 8/6/94: <In order to have a society of any sort, there must
be some sort of limited "groupism."> and <I am driven to think that the formal
structure is not terribly important; rather, it is the spirit of the individuals
composing the group.>

Our absolute connections are expressed in a degree of consensual purpose, the
prevalent spirit of Individualism makes the best consensus.

