From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Sun, 7 Aug 1994 00:25:55 -0400
Subject: [#94-8-776169964] Infomorph ethics

   I just read Charles Platt's article "Two questions for Extropians"
in Extropy # 13.  Among other things, Charles discusses the possible
ethical code for infomorphs - the uploaded/informational beings, and
possible levels of harm to them, ranging form total loss of data
(info-death) to alteration of a single bit, and suggests to base the
ethical code on the quantity and quality of data corruption.
 
   I like the term 'infomorph', and find the topic of transhuman social
life very interesting.
 
   However, I happen to disagree with Charles in what it may look like.
 
   Any sufficiently smart infomorph would get one of those standard
programs that would allow it to keep numerous encrypted copies of itself
in lots of safe places, so any corruption of data would be absolutely
impossible.
 
  However, an infomorh can still be harmed, although not 'personally'.
One can damage its possessions, reputation or rights, or interfere with 
its interests and opportunities.
Many of these things may be restorable, but this will require expenses.
 
   Methinks it unlikely that the informorphs will have cheap and ample
resources of material possessions and computational power, as this would
mean that they will have no interest or imagination to put their (vast,
but still limited) resources to use.    I would rather expect that the
available resources will always grow slower than desired resources  and,
with increasing ability of advanced beings to put them to use, will command
higher and higher premiums.
 
   So I would expect infomorphs to build their ethical code on access rights
and economic privileges but not on 'personal' safety.
 

