From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Date: Sun, 7 Aug 1994 00:53:19 -0700
Subject: [#94-8-776169965] Reincarnation, immortality and cryonic suspension

>When do extropians want to be reanimated?:
>
>1. When the cause of their death is cured, but old age has not been cured.
No.

>2. When reanimation is possible, aging is cured, but uploading is not possible.
>3. When reanimation is possible, old age is cured, and uploading is possible.
Both fine by me.

>4. When reanimation is 25% successful.  50%?  90%?  99%?
Give me 99+, thanks.  I'll wait a while.

>5. When reanimation is possible, but not with the same body.
>6. When reanimation is possible, but only with the same body.
I prefer to start out with my own body -- I want the choice of whether or
not to switch.  I'd only go for 5 if there were determined to be some
fundamental reason why 6 wouldn't work.

>7. When reanimation is possible, but with side-effects.
Like what?  If it lowers my IQ, forget it.  Any other physical stuff I
figure would be fixed by the time they can return my frozen,
cryoprotectant-laced body to functionality.

>8. When reanimation is possible for you, but not your life-partner yet.
Assuming I had one, I think I'd say yes, because then I could work towards
getting him/her reanimated.

>9. When reanimation is possible, but only by assuming a massive debt.
How massive are we talking?   In general, I'd say yes, and assume I could
pay it off.  Then again, I'd be interested in the trend of the debt
involved.  If the procedure gets (say) half as expensive every 10 years,
I'd tend to wait till paying it off would require less than about 5 years
of my labor.

>10. When reanimation is possible, but society considers your religion/sexual
>preference/radical politics to be criminal, and you will be sentenced to life
>imprisonment upon reanimation.
Given that I plan to live indefinitely, I'd say it would be impractical on
"society's" part, but I'd say no anyway.  Then again, if the above were the
case, most likely either the cryonics organization personnel would already
have been imprisoned, in which case I'm SOL, or they would have experience
with how to keep from getting caught which they would presumably be willing
to share with me -- "for a fee", as Fred says.

>11. When reanimation is possible, but a desired goal (space travel, money fund
>peaks, etc.) has not yet occurred.
As an agent of change, I'd certainly rather be working towards whatever
goal it is than lying around "waiting" for it to happen.

