Date: Mon, 8 Aug 1994 01:24:40 -0400
Subject: [#94-8-776169969] Infomorph ethics

Craig Presson <fcp@nuance.com> writes:
 
>In <199408070425.AA18172@eris.cs.umb.edu>, Alexander Chislenko writes:
 
>>    Any sufficiently smart infomorph would get one of those standard
>> programs that would allow it to keep numerous encrypted copies of itself
>> in lots of safe places, so any corruption of data would be absolutely
>> impossible.
 
>Actually, you arm-waved just a little there. It would be just possible
>to do what you said but at a large cost in verification and restoration
>of corrupted copies.
 
  I was partly using Charles Platt's assumption of cheap resources
to argue with his suggestion of infomorphs' personal vulnerability.
Also, out of all info-processes infomorphs could engage in, I would
expect copying and passive storage to be the cheapest.
So if anything may be affordable to an infomorph, personal safety will.
 
> OTOH, the simple case of hiding the last copy is easier to ensure and
>makes murder nearly impossible.
 
  So you do agree?   I'd just note that multiple copies and data
encryption offer exponential increase in safety at a linear increase
in cost.
 
>To make a long story short, "who" is doing the checking for corruption
>and how do you know that program is not corrupted?
 
  If I (the infomorph) cypher all my data with an unbreakable key, nobody
would be able to switch a single bit in me without my noticing it.
Same with possessions.  Whoever owns the data, protects it and can easily
tell if it were attacked.
  A more difficult case is if I was forced (how? I feel no pain, unless
I want to...), bribed or tricked into corrupting my own data and giving
up my archives. I could fight against that by creating archives that would
get back to me themselves, without my knowing how to find them.
If the 'corrupted me' keeps resisting and fighting off the archived
'true selves', it's still not death of the original self - it does live
in the archives.
 
   One may still expect a high infomorph mortality rate for economic
reasons: if you can't maintain resources for yourself and your archives,
they get taken by other beings.   With all available computational
space densely populated by extremely complex info-structures, each of
them capable of generating any amount of its own copies and other
systems, any chunk of information in existence will have to have some
*reason* (and secure the means) for its upkeep.
   Unless the infomorphs figure out how to expand the available space at
will, their ability to grow will far outstrip the rate of resource expansion.
In such conditions, much attention will be paid to better utilization
of available resources and reuse of the information carriers currently
possessed by 'junk' structures - each of them, after a certain point,
much more advanced than any human and, probably, unwilling to be erazed.
 
 
  I'm quite sure that this is not a very accurate account of infomorphs'
social life - just some human speculation.
 
   Sasha.

