From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Mon, 8 Aug 94 14:10:52 EDT
Subject: [#94-8-776169970] Individualism vs Groupism (was FAQ 0.05)

> Reilly Jones:
> >Other identifiers of Groupism are:
> > credentialism (extremely debilitating to Pan-Critical Rational processes),

Nick Szabo replies-

> Can you elaborate on this?  Credentials are one of the main ways
> we communicate reputation in a world bigger than a small tribe.  
> A credentialing mechanism forms the centerpiece of David Chaum's 
> privacy-enhanced social mileau, the inspiriation for crypto-anarchy.

In fact (ironically) I like to summarize Chaum's important "making 
Big Brother Obsolete" argument as: Credentials, not Identity.  (Or,
Authenticity, not Identity.)  Meaning, we can and should design 
systems so that you can prove what someone needs to know about you without 
giving away who you are.

But in a way this just emphasizes how important identity is--that you
shouldn't reveal it lightly.

Another slightly-off-kilter response to Reilly and his wonderful Emerson
quote [the machine I'm typing on, "waldo," is named after Emerson; I
work in Concord, MA]: as a sort of Taoist, I would say it's not so much
a "non-anchored self-identity" that's the problem as the compulsion to 
anchor that drives people to identify with groups, places, or ideas
(Extropianism or individualism, even).  Why we are so keen to have an
identity I'm not sure I've ever seen a good explanation of.  (I remember
finding "The True Believer" opaque and grouchy.)  Maybe it has to do
with being expected to provide consistent accounts of oneself.
Daniel Dennet(t?) says this is a central part of the trick we've 
learned called "consciousness."  I think it's part of being expected to 
provide "right" answers to questions.  Maybe it also has to do with that 
section of the brain that goes nuts in obsessive-compulsive disorder,
checking and rechecking that everything's in order.  ("Let's see, my
name is so-and-so, check.  I'm a Red Sox fan, check.  I like sushi, 
check.  Didn't forget my wallet, check...all systems go.")

